**Case and dockets:** Slack v. McDaniel; No. 98-6322.
**Event and date:** Plenary merits and threshold decision; 2000-04-26; October Term 1999.
**Result:** Ninth Circuit certificate denial reversed and remanded, 7–2; amended certificate regime applies, 6–3, with a five-Justice rationale; no certificate or writ issued.
**Version / lineage:** Original decision; no prior adjudication replaced. Repository verification and commitment reserved to the operator.

**Source chunk:** OT_1999CHUNK5.

## Event, participation and entering law

On certiorari under 28 U.S.C. §1254(1) to the Ninth Circuit's July 7, 1998 order denying a certificate of probable cause. Argued October 4, 1999; reargued March 29, 2000; decided April 26 under the authorized calendar. The underlying District Court disposition was March 31, 1998. All nine participated at argument, reargument and decision; no disqualification is established. Quorum six and judgment threshold five are satisfied. Research cutoff: immediately before April 26, 2000. The live questions are judicial review of the certificate denial, the applicable certificate regime, the threshold for a procedural-dismissal appeal and the status of the exhaustion-return application including added grounds. The Court does not review an already authorized merits appeal or an actual circuit successive-application authorization determination.

Slack's Nevada second-degree-murder conviction with a deadly weapon produced consecutive life terms with possible parole; the case is noncapital. His direct appeal was dismissed November 5, 1991. He filed his first personal federal petition November 27, 1991, raising sufficiency, sexual-relationship evidence, reasonable doubt and premeditation issues, then sought abeyance to exhaust additional matters. After a telephonic hearing, with neither side objecting, the District Court dismissed without prejudice February 19, 1992 and allowed application to renew after exhaustion. There was no federal merits adjudication and no finding of deliberate abandonment or withholding. State postconviction proceedings began in July 1992.

The District Court and principal brief identify commencement of the renewed federal action on March 27, 1995; the reproduced petition's filing date is May 30, 1995. Those are different recorded events, both before April 24, 1996. The isolated May 20 wording supplies no third verified date. Counsel was appointed February 13, 1997 with instructions to investigate potential grounds and amend; the third amended petition was filed December 24, 1997. The March abuse order is dated March 10 in its text and March 11 in the appendix contents. Neither date difference affects an operative holding, and no source discrepancy is silently resolved.

The abuse order found adequate State pleading as to grounds 2, 4(c), 5, 6 and 7 and directed a cause-and-prejudice or fundamental-miscarriage response within fifteen days. Slack sought reconsideration of the successive classification instead of supplying the directed excuse showing. On March 31 the District Court dismissed those five grounds with prejudice as abusive under Rule 9(b), separately found ground 3 unexhausted and dismissed the action without prejudice on that basis. It allowed a thirty-day reopening request with personally signed abandonment of ground 3, or further state exhaustion, and discussed new filing and limitations consequences. This Court does not restart that period or adopt an unreviewed limitations conclusion.

| Ground | Claim | Actual district treatment |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Insufficient evidence of second-degree murder | Not among the five abuse dismissals; within the action dismissed for nonexhaustion |
| 2 | Insufficient notice from open-murder charging instrument | With prejudice as abusive; State presentation acknowledged lacking |
| 3 | Sexual-misconduct evidence as federal due-process error | Unexhausted; separate basis for whole-action dismissal without prejudice |
| 4(a), 4(b) | Reasonable doubt; premeditation and deliberation instructions | Not included in the abuse dismissal |
| 4(c) | Malice-aforethought instruction | With prejudice as abusive; State presentation acknowledged lacking |
| 5 | Trial counsel's evidence/instruction objections, investigation and defenses | With prejudice as abusive |
| 6 | Appellate counsel's omitted notice/instruction claims and concession of issues | With prejudice as abusive |
| 7 | Cumulative error | With prejudice as abusive |

The State's alternative exhaustion objections to the five abuse-dismissed grounds remained undecided. Notice of appeal was filed April 29, 1998; the CPC request and District denial were dated May 11, with the denial filed May 12. The District applied Barefoot's substantial-federal-showing and reasonable-debate standard and found no substantial appellate issue. The Ninth Circuit's July 7 denial supplied no substantive explanation. No certificate has issued. A later Supreme Court merits-review event is not an adjudication that the underlying constitutional claims are substantial or valid.

The Slack slices and PROCEDURE supplement supply Hohn's certificate-denial review holding and express transition reservation; Lindh/Carriger's identified district-law temporal rules; Felker's actual authorization-review bar; Peguero's actually issued certificate; Lonchar's lawful-predicate requirement; and Stewart's narrow presented, premature and preserved Ford-claim rule. The operative section 2253, Rule 22, former section 2244 and Rule 9 text was read from mechanically copied sources. The current-term supplement preserves Weeks's independent gates. Actual Michael Williams, Terry Williams and Edwards Public Projections were read; the last uses the corrected join list. They supply no Nevada default, factual-development fault or new application-date rule. The independent same-day Beck opinion supplies the assignment count, not a substantive habeas premise.

