**Standing fallback used — certificate-regime transition component:** The supplement does not expressly choose whether the pre-enactment district application or post-enactment appeal fixes the certificate regime. Under the standing fallback, Stone joins the majority disposition applying amended section 2253(c) to this 1998 appeal and adds no ground. He does not join Part II's appellate-date rationale, is not assigned a contrary view of Lindh, and is not eligible to author the complete lead on the basis of that vote. The fallback supplies no missing reason for a required Section 10 statement because this is not such an application. The public projection identifies his agreement on the regime component and absence of a Part II join without disclosing private instructions.

No other fallback, invented procedural premise or additional Stone rationale is used. His requested remand does not issue a certificate, a writ, a hearing, a new thirty-day period or a reset limitations period. The exact current-law refresh changes no approved substantive core.

## Form, assignment and final compatibility

The root operator fixed the four-part signed form in the separate receipt before any count. Assembly independently recounted the actual Records after Beck. Part I review and Part III's conditional certificate rule have all nine joins. Part II's appellate-date rationale has O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Thomas and Ginsburg; Stone agrees with the regime disposition only, making six, while Stevens, Souter and Breyer retain former CPC. Part IV classification and reversal have Stone, Stevens, O'Connor, Kennedy, Souter, Ginsburg and Breyer; Scalia and Thomas oppose. The Chief belongs to each tentative judgment coalition and assigns the lead, but his judgment-only transition vote supplies no full-author eligibility.

**Fresh counts before assignment:** Stone-Zsela 6; Stevens 5; O'Connor 6; Scalia 6; Kennedy 6; Souter 6; Thomas 6; Ginsburg 6; Breyer 6. Last counted event: Beck v. Prupis, 2000-04-26; Stone-Zsela.

Eligible authors for the complete opinion: O'Connor, Kennedy and Ginsburg. Each supports every proposition in all four parts. All three have six and all three form the balance pool. The other Justices' partial or judgment-only joins do not qualify them for the complete lead. No legal ground requires a divided authorship when three eligible authors can carry it. Ginsburg writes, count six. Fit governs the precise separation of the reviewable judicial certificate denial from actual authorization review and the new appellate-transition holding from unchanged district merits law. Her Hohn opinion expressly preserved the transition issue, and her Lindh opinion states the provision-specific application-date rule that must remain intact. Those concrete prior explanations position her to state what the present holding adds without claiming either question was already settled. Kennedy, also six, is the strongest alternative because his Hohn, Felker and Stewart joins support the same distinctions. Ginsburg's authorship of the two immediately implicated temporal/review precedents supplies the comparative fit advantage. No new accommodation or changed commitment is needed, and Stone's incomplete rationale join precludes self-assignment of the complete lead.

Stevens writes the concurrence in part and in the judgment, joined by Souter and Breyer, preserving the whole-pending-case CPC construction while joining Parts I, III and IV. Thomas writes the partial concurrence and dissent, joined by Scalia, preserving the independently operative new-ground abuse branch and affirmance; both join Parts I–III. These selections rest on the recorded competing constructions, not historical authorship. Neither separate opinion counts.

The reconciliation's explicit temporal correction governs over the provisional model: Stevens, Souter and Breyer do not support the appellate-date rationale. Their conditional Part III join is not a present-regime join. The same handoff clarifies reversal, rather than mere reconsideration of an unsettled classification, because the seven conclusively reject the successive ground. All final component joins match the corrected commitments. No further vote change, fictional draft exchange, new misconduct finding or historical Chief vote is supplied. One signed plenary lead accrues to Ginsburg after this Record is written.

